{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"c4f93b78d5c176814c7919cfe8e6e9a9d53a5ecf7dd631b23ac2aea0d2028fdb","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_65736915906153d0d9cdf54182b4bed482b2fa5a28010cf14cb2444b8b2cd4ac","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a theoretically grounded framework connecting the Lagrangian formulation of optimization to symmetry breaking in deep learning. The methodological approach is mathematically consistent, deriving a generalized Noether's theorem that accounts for kinetic asymmetry. The experimental validation is targeted and effectively supports the specific theoretical predictions regarding the role of momentum and weight decay in implicit adaptive optimization, though the scope is limited to a single architecture and dataset.","paper_id":"fiPtD7iXuhn","paper_identity_sha256":"c8b225903266775d1733f084b6d8a5a9f54709497a049518cea78440ab4fbd0e","prompt_identity_sha256":"08f115f582d802d132efe784b14d8f601899aaf5aad5a8f8ab9e2a66b0348cd3","raw_response_sha256":"bde1c946a041adc72e336001ccce050c40d92aa9dafc20c592452b151186b073","request_identity_sha256":"8f82ba7f7a8d3ec33844a69759fa9a7acf680dbbe5764dec502a52f519537b44","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"187834b9ad1d8df2a242a375e8f7fec963907e863318d1ca157afc49dc30d95d","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-016","SRC-017","SRC-024","SRC-034"],"review_text":"The proposed method is technically sound and mathematically consistent. The authors successfully bridge the gap between discrete optimization and continuous-time Lagrangian dynamics by utilizing modified equation analysis to show that gradient descent with a finite learning rate corresponds to a second-order differential equation. The derivation of the generalized Noether's theorem, which explicitly accounts for kinetic asymmetry, is logically rigorous. The application of this theory to BatchNorm layers to derive an exact solution for the effective learning rate (Eq. 17) is a strong technical contribution that correctly identifies the analogy to RMSProp.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-039","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The experimental design is adequate for validating the specific theoretical claims made in the paper. The authors employ a well-motivated ablation study where the filter norm is constrained to test the hypothesis that symmetry breaking (via unconstrained norm dynamics) is beneficial. The experiments on VGG11 with Tiny ImageNet effectively demonstrate that the advantage of implicit adaptive optimization is present in high learning rate and high momentum regimes, as predicted by the theory. However, the evaluation is limited to a single architecture (VGG11) and a single dataset (Tiny ImageNet), which restricts the generalizability of the empirical findings.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-033","SRC-039"],"review_text":"The paper provides sufficient detail for the theoretical reproduction of the derived equations, including the specific hyperparameter regimes (e.g., learning rate eta ~ 10^-2, weight decay k ~ 10^-4) used to justify the overdamped approximation. The experimental protocol for the 'constrained filter norm' ablation is clearly described, specifying the operation q(t) <- q(t)/|q(t)| |q(0)|. However, the paper does not explicitly state the availability of code or provide a comprehensive list of all hyperparameters (such as batch size, number of epochs, or specific initialization schemes) required to fully replicate the training runs shown in Figure 3.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-037","SRC-038"],"review_text":"The paper effectively positions its work relative to prior literature on the geometry of optimization and the benefits of normalization layers. It correctly identifies and contrasts its findings with previous works that treated symmetry breaking as an obstacle, offering a counterexample where it is beneficial. The comparison with RMSProp is a strong point of comparison, as the authors derive a continuous-time description of RMSProp to show an exact functional match with the implicit dynamics induced by BatchNorm. The related work section adequately covers the relevant state-of-the-art in natural gradient and batch normalization theory.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-040"],"review_text":"The paper relies on deterministic theoretical derivations and single-run empirical observations for its validation. While the theoretical results are exact within the model's assumptions, the experimental results in Figure 3 do not report error bars, confidence intervals, or results from multiple random seeds. Given the stochastic nature of deep learning training, the absence of statistical validation makes it difficult to assess the variance of the reported improvements in test accuracy and loss dynamics, particularly for the smaller differences observed in the low learning rate regime.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"03172b5107792e7998ff9c8bf6ccd493c228ba5931a294c81bcf2f259871b598","paper_id":"fiPtD7iXuhn","paper_ordinal":114,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"bde1c946a041adc72e336001ccce050c40d92aa9dafc20c592452b151186b073","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"fiPtD7iXuhn","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"68f6ee404c5984f7587c422204464971ebfa68ec5dfb4de6499016055219c82b","request_identity_sha256":"8f82ba7f7a8d3ec33844a69759fa9a7acf680dbbe5764dec502a52f519537b44","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":987,"prompt_tokens":18675,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":19662},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"8f82ba7f7a8d3ec33844a69759fa9a7acf680dbbe5764dec502a52f519537b44","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}